---
layout: base.njk
title: Notes on job hunting
description: Short, practical posts. New ones added over time.
---
{#- Screen 2e. As on 2a, 2b and 2c, title and description are the design's own h1
    and lead, used verbatim rather than written fresh.

    No contactFooter here, which is what gives this page the compact dark footer
    the design draws rather than the homepage's full contact block.

    No permalink either — Eleventy maps this file to /blog/, which is what the nav
    already points at, and what makes aria-current="page" fire in header.njk.

    The mockup's two dashed "future post" cards are deliberately not here. They
    illustrate that the list grows by itself; they are not content, and an empty
    card is not something a visitor should be shown. -#}

<div class="page-head">
  <h1 class="page-head__title">Notes on job hunting</h1>
  <p class="page-head__lead">Short, practical posts. New ones added over time.</p>
</div>

{#- collections.post is every file in src/posts, gathered by the tag in
    src/posts/posts.json — so Sarah publishes by adding a file and nothing in here
    changes. Eleventy builds a collection oldest first, hence reverse for the
    newest-first order the design shows. Nunjucks' reverse copies the array before
    reversing it, so this cannot disturb the collection for any other page.

    A ul where the design has bare divs: this is a list of posts, and saying so
    gets "list, N items" announced first — the same call testimonials.njk and
    fees.njk made. -#}
<ul class="posts">
  {%- for post in collections.post | reverse %}

  {#- The date is a <time> so it is machine-readable as well as visible. Both
      filters read the same front matter value: postDate gives the design's
      "12 Aug 2026", isoDate the datetime attribute a machine wants.

      The link sits inside the h2 rather than wrapping the whole card, so a screen
      reader announces it as the post title alone instead of the date, title and
      description run together. The card is still tappable edge to edge — that is
      the stretched ::after in pages.css. -#}
  <li class="card post-card">
    <time class="post-card__date" datetime="{{ post.date | isoDate }}">{{ post.date | postDate }}</time>
    <h2 class="card__title">
      <a class="post-card__link" href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.data.title }}</a>
    </h2>
    <p class="card__note">{{ post.data.description }}</p>
  </li>
  {%- endfor %}
</ul>
